  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nThe forecast begins today with upper level northwest flow with a\nwarming trend continuing into Tuesday.\n\nA cold frontal passage late Tuesday will lead to evening \nthunderstorms, and continued showers and potential thunder into \nWednesday. At this time, HREF is showing a few storms in the NW\nflow could bring wind gusts of 60-65 mph and large hail. SPC has\nupgraded the NE two-thirds of the CWA to a Marginal Risk of severe\nthunderstorms. These look to mainly occur from 4 pm-9 pm. With \nthe jet stream overhead of these storms, it won't take much to \nget a gust to 60 mph.\n\nThere could be a minor concern for Fire Weather issues in the \nsouthwest zones late Tuesday but fuels have been too green lately\nfor major concerns.\n\nIncreasing west winds behind the front may also necessitate the\na Lake Wind Advisory for Fort Peck Lake.\n\nWednesday's storms could be similar as this W-NW flow is quick.\nWinds could be the better threat at this point. (HREF not able to\nshow this yet)\n\nA quiet period can be expected Thursday-Friday but an upper trough\nmoves in from the Pacific NW this weekend that looks to bring\nanother round of thunderstorms.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nPops through Tuesday were blended with the latest CAMS. NBM has\nbeen too dry lately with this cyclonic NW flow.\n\nThe rest of the forecast used the NBM. TFJ",
